   "factsOk": true,
   "ambiguous": false,
   "symbols": [
    "ValueConcurrentObserver",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.start()",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.syncStart",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.asyncStart",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.asyncStartObservation",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.syncStartWithoutWALSnapshot",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.asyncStartWithoutWALSnapshot",
    "ValueConcurrentObserver.startObservation",
    "DatabasePool._add",
    "DatabasePool._addConcurrent",
    "DatabasePool.walSnapshotTransaction",
    "WALSnapshotTransaction",
    "WALSnapshotTransaction.init",
    "WALSnapshotTransaction.read",
    "WALSnapshotTransaction.close",
    "WALSnapshot",
    "WALSnapshot.init",
    "WALSnapshot.compare",
    "Database.setUpWALMode",
    "SQLITE_ENABLE_SNAPSHOT",
    "SQLITE_DISABLE_SNAPSHOT"
   ],
   "claims": [
    "supported",
    "supported",
    "supported",
    "supported",
    "supported",
    "supported"
   ],
   "why": "DatabasePool._add dispatches to _addConcurrent, which creates ValueConcurrentObserver; start() can call syncStart or asyncStart. Both first fetch from a reader, not the writer: syncStart/asyncStart obtain a long-lived WALSnapshotTransaction from DatabasePool.walSnapshotTransaction(), which opens a deferred read transaction on a reader and records a WALSnapshot of that moment. The initial value is fetched/reduced/notified from that reader snapshot, so it can arrive even during a long write. Then asyncStartObservation runs on the writer queue, takes a WALSnapshot of the writer connection, and compares it to the initial snapshot via WALSnapshot.compare (sqlite3_snapshot_cmp). If the writer snapshot is newer, or cannot be taken, the observer assumes a change, refetches from the writer, and notifies a fresh value; otherwise it just starts observing. The initial WALSnapshotTransaction is closed after the comparison, returning its reader to the pool. Fallback: without SQLITE_ENABLE_SNAPSHOT, or with SQLITE_DISABLE_SNAPSHOT (defined for Linux in Package.swift), the #else path routes to syncStartWithoutWALSnapshot/asyncStartWithoutWALSnapshot, which cannot compare versions and therefore always perform a second writer fetch after observation starts, potentially notifying the same value twice. At runtime, if snapshot creation throws SQLITE_ERROR (missing/empty WAL file), the observer degrades to the same without-snapshot path; Database.setUpWALMode creates and drops a small table to ensure the WAL file is non-empty."
  }
 },
